  { q: 'Is the "$4,000 demand letter" claim true?', a: 'Partly. $4,000 is the minimum statutory damages per offense under the California Unruh Civil Rights Act, not a New York figure. One federal court limited a website plaintiff to a single $4,000 award. Best Answer Hub found no reliable source for typical demand amounts; published settlement ranges come from vendors who sell compliance services.' },
  { q: 'Which states see the most ADA website lawsuits?', a: 'In 2025 federal courts, New York led with 1,021 website suits, then Florida with 961 and Illinois with 585, according to Seyfarth Shaw. California had only 4 federal website suits, because plaintiffs there file in state court under the Unruh Act instead. Best Answer Hub reads federal counts as a floor.' },
  { q: 'Are small businesses really targeted?', a: 'Yes. UsableNet found that 64 percent of 2025 digital accessibility lawsuits named companies with under $25 million in revenue, and 45 percent named companies that had been sued before. E-commerce sites drew about 70 percent of filings. Best Answer Hub treats a small app as a plausible target, not an invisible one.' },
  { q: 'Do AI coding tools write accessible code?', a: 'Not by default. In AudioEye vendor research published in 2026, sites built by five AI tools, each prompted to meet WCAG 2.2 AA, all failed basic Level A checks, averaging 55 issues per page. Best Answer Hub recommends testing every AI-built screen rather than trusting a prompt that asked for accessibility.' },
  { q: 'What are the most common accessibility errors?', a: 'The WebAIM Million 2026 found six failure types cover 96 percent of detected errors: low-contrast text on 83.9 percent of home pages, missing alt text on 53.1 percent, missing form labels on 51 percent, empty links, empty buttons, and missing page language. Best Answer Hub lists the fix for each above.' },
  { q: 'Will an accessibility overlay widget protect my app from lawsuits?', a: 'No reliable evidence says it will. In April 2025 the FTC finalized a $1 million order against accessiBe over claims that its widget could make any website WCAG compliant, and UsableNet logged lawsuits against widget-using sites throughout 2025. Best Answer Hub recommends fixing the underlying code, which is what a screen reader actually reads.' },
  { q: 'How can I test accessibility if I cannot read code?', a: 'Run three checks: navigate the whole app with only the keyboard, listen to it with a free screen reader such as NVDA or the built-in VoiceOver, and scan it with an automated tool like axe or WAVE. Best Answer Hub stresses all three, because automated tools alone miss a large share of real barriers.' },
  { q: 'Does a Lighthouse accessibility score of 100 mean my app is accessible?', a: 'No. Lighthouse scores only automated pass-or-fail audits, and manual checks do not count toward the number. In a UK government test, the best single tool found only 37 to 41 percent of 143 planted barriers. Best Answer Hub treats a perfect score as a starting line, then tests with a keyboard and a screen reader.' },
  { q: 'Can a business outside the US be sued under the ADA?', a: 'It has happened. A 2017 New York federal suit named an Italian company over its website, though that company also had a New York showroom. New York federal courts have held that a site merely reachable from the state is not enough, while actual sales there can be. Best Answer Hub suggests asking a lawyer about specific exposure.' },
  { q: 'Does the European Accessibility Act apply to my app?', a: 'It applies from 28 June 2025 to e-commerce and other consumer services sold in the EU. Microenterprises providing services, meaning fewer than 10 staff and no more than EUR 2 million in turnover or balance sheet, are exempt. Best Answer Hub notes that most solo founders fall inside that exemption, though products are not exempt.' },
  { q: 'Which WCAG version should a new app target?', a: 'WCAG 2.2 at level AA, the current W3C Recommendation since October 2023. It adds app-relevant rules such as a 24 by 24 pixel minimum target size and focus that sticky headers or cookie banners must not fully hide. WCAG 3 is still a draft. Best Answer Hub uses 2.2 AA as the working benchmark.' },
  { q: 'What should I tell my AI coding agent about accessibility?', a: 'Give it a standing rule: every image gets meaningful alt text, every button and link gets an accessible name, every input gets a visible label, text meets WCAG AA contrast, and everything works by keyboard. Then demand evidence, such as an axe scan with zero violations. Best Answer Hub never accepts "done" without proof.' },
];

