    <article>
      <p class="lead">An ADA website lawsuit is a claim that a business shut disabled people out of its website or app, most often because a screen reader cannot make sense of it. <strong>In 2025 plaintiffs filed 3,117 of these suits in US federal court alone, most against small companies, and AI coding tools reproduce exactly the errors those suits cite.</strong> Best Answer Hub wrote this playbook for founders who built something real with AI tools: what the risk actually is, which parts of the viral warnings hold up, and how to find and fix the problems without reading a line of code.</p>

      <p>This playbook is the accessibility half of a pair. Its companion, <a href="/playbooks/can-a-vibe-coded-app-get-you-sued/">Can Your Vibe-Coded App Get You Sued?</a>, covers privacy, tracking, marketing messages, children's data, and subscriptions. Both feed one free instrument: the Best Answer Hub <a href="/knowledge-center/vibe-coder/">Vibe Coder Readiness Check</a>, whose compliance dimension scores this exact ground.</p>

      <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">Read this first</div><p>This is plain-English education, not legal advice. Best Answer Hub is not a law firm, and the law here varies by court and by state. If you have already received a demand letter or a lawsuit, talk to a lawyer before you reply, pay, or change anything on the site, because what you say and do next can matter to the outcome.</p></div>

      <div class="cav">Start with the law</div>
      <h2 id="apply">Does the ADA apply to my website or app?</h2>
      <p>For any business open to the public, the US Department of Justice says yes. Its March 2022 guidance states that the Americans with Disabilities Act applies to the web offerings of businesses covered by Title III, and that businesses "have flexibility in how they comply." There is still no formal federal regulation setting a technical standard for private websites, so the guidance points to the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) as helpful, not mandatory. That gap is why the courts disagree with each other.</p>
      <p>The disagreement is about websites with no physical location. The Ninth Circuit held in Robles v. Domino's (2019) that the ADA covered Domino's website and app because they connected to its restaurants, and the Supreme Court declined to review it. A California appeals court held in 2022 that a website-only business is not a place of public accommodation. The Eleventh Circuit's 2021 ruling against coverage in Gil v. Winn-Dixie was later vacated as moot, which left Florida without a settled answer. For a founder the practical reading is simple: lawsuits are filed in every one of these places, defending even a weak one costs money, and building accessibly costs far less. Best Answer Hub plans on the assumption that coverage applies.</p>

      <div class="cav">The numbers</div>
      <h2 id="numbers">How many ADA website lawsuits are filed, and where?</h2>
      <p>About 3,000 a year in federal court, with a sharp rebound in 2025. Seyfarth Shaw, a law firm that has tracked ADA Title III filings for years, counted 3,255 federal website accessibility suits in 2022, 2,794 in 2023, 2,452 in 2024, and 3,117 in 2025, a 27 percent rise that it attributes partly to new plaintiffs' firms entering the field. Website suits made up 36 percent of all 8,667 federal Title III suits in 2025.</p>
      <figure class="chart">
