      </figure>
      <p>Federal counts are a floor, not the full picture. UsableNet, an accessibility vendor that also tracks New York and California state courts, counted more than 5,000 digital accessibility lawsuits in 2025 across federal and state courts. Its report adds three numbers that matter to a small founder: 64 percent of suits named companies with under $25 million in revenue, 45 percent named companies that had already been sued before, and e-commerce sites drew about 70 percent of filings. Demand letters, the step before a suit, are not counted by anyone, and Seyfarth notes that a significant number never reach a court at all.</p>
      <p>Geography is concentrated. In 2025 federal website suits, New York led with 1,021, Florida followed with 961, and Illinois with 585. California logged just 4, which looks like safety and is not: plaintiffs there file in state court under the Unruh Civil Rights Act, which carries its own damages. Activity has kept climbing in 2026, and UsableNet projects roughly 6,000 digital suits for the full year, about a fifth more than 2025. Best Answer Hub reads all of this as one message: the volume is real, and it lands mostly on small sites.</p>

      <div class="cav">The fact-check</div>
      <h2 id="viral">Is the viral "$4,000 letter from New York" claim true?</h2>
      <p>Two of its three claims hold up, and the money detail is borrowed from another state. A short video circulating among vibe coders says a demand letter "from New York" asks for $4,000, that over 3,000 such cases reached federal court last year, and that New York and Florida filed the most. The table below checks each claim against the primary data.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>The viral claim</th><th>Verdict</th><th>What the record shows</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="band">"Over 3,000 went to federal court last year"</td><td><span class="score lv1">True</span></td><td>3,117 federal website suits in 2025 (Seyfarth Shaw); 3,195 federal digital suits (UsableNet).</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">"New York and Florida filed the most"</td><td><span class="score lv1">True for 2025</span></td><td>New York 1,021 and Florida 961 federal website suits in 2025. Counting all Title III suits in early 2026, California leads.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">"A letter from New York asking for $4,000"</td><td><span class="score lv2">Misattributed</span></td><td>$4,000 is California's Unruh Act minimum per offense. No New York statute sets that figure.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">"A picture with no label is enough for a letter"</td><td><span class="score lv1">Plausible</span></td><td>Missing alt text appears on 53.1% of top home pages (WebAIM, 2026) and is a standard complaint item.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">"Test it with a screen reader"</td><td><span class="score lv1">Good advice</span></td><td>Automated tools miss a large share of barriers; a free screen reader catches what they cannot.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>The $4,000 figure comes from California Civil Code section 52, which sets damages "in no case less than four thousand dollars" for each offense under the Unruh Act, and an ADA violation counts as an Unruh violation. Plaintiffs once argued that meant $4,000 per visit; in the Domino's case a federal court treated the inaccessible website as one overarching violation and limited the award to $4,000. New York state and city human rights laws allow damages too, but they set no $4,000 floor. Typical settlement amounts are harder to pin down: the published ranges, often quoted between $5,000 and $20,000 for a website case, come from vendors who sell compliance services, and Best Answer Hub found no independent data behind them.</p>

      <div class="cav">The AI factor</div>
      <h2 id="ai">Do AI coding tools write accessible code?</h2>
      <p>Not by default, even when asked to. AudioEye, an accessibility vendor, asked five AI tools (from OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, xAI, and Lovable) to build three sites each, a news site, a store, and a financial services site, all to WCAG 2.2 level AA. All 15 sites failed basic Level A checks. The study logged 306 distinct issues occurring more than 59,000 times, an average of 55 issues per AI-built page, with a third of issues blocking a task outright and nearly half visible only when the site is actually used. It is vendor research, published in 2026, and Best Answer Hub labels it that way, but its direction matches independent work.</p>
      <p>That independent work points the same way. The WebAIM Million, an annual scan of the top one million home pages, found detectable WCAG failures on 95.9 percent of them in 2026, up from 94.8 percent, with average errors per page rising 10 percent to 56.1. WebAIM links the rise to heavier frameworks and AI-assisted "vibe coding," which is its interpretation rather than a direct measurement. A 2026 academic study of 300 interfaces generated by Claude, Gemini, and GPT models found about 1.8 semantic accessibility problems per interface that automated checkers pass, such as images labeled simply "image."</p>
      <div class="callout sage"><div class="h">The honest counterweight</div><p>Language models are not uniquely bad at this. A 2025 comparative study found they often produce more accessible code than human developers on basics such as color contrast and alt text, while struggling with complex patterns like ARIA attributes. The lesson is not "AI ruins accessibility." It is that a prompt asking for accessibility is a request, not a result, and that the person who ships the app owns the outcome either way.</p></div>

      <div class="cav">The checklist</div>
      <h2 id="six">What do ADA demand letters actually target?</h2>
      <p>Mostly the same six errors, because they are the ones automated scans find on almost every site. The WebAIM Million 2026 reports that 96 percent of all detected errors fall into six categories, and they map closely to the barriers complaints describe: a screen reader user who cannot tell what a button does, cannot fill in a form, or cannot read a low-contrast price.</p>
