        <p class="chart-src">Source: WebAIM Million, 2026 edition (data from February 2026). These six categories account for 96 percent of all detected errors.</p>
      </figure>
      <p>Each one has a plain-English fix and a one-line instruction for a coding agent. Best Answer Hub recommends handing the agent all six as a standing rule rather than one at a time, then asking for proof.</p>
      <div class="sec">
        <ul class="pillars">
          <li><span class="dot">1</span><div><b>Low-contrast text (83.9 percent).</b> Gray text on a white background, or white text on a pale button, that low-vision users cannot read. WCAG AA asks for a 4.5 to 1 contrast ratio for normal text. Tell the agent: "Make every text and background pair meet WCAG AA contrast." The free Best Answer Hub <a href="/tools/color-palette-generator/">Color Palette Generator</a> checks contrast before you pick a color.</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">2</span><div><b>Missing alt text (53.1 percent).</b> Images a screen reader announces as nothing, or as a file name. Tell the agent: "Give every meaningful image alt text that says what it shows, and mark decorative images with empty alt."</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">3</span><div><b>Missing form labels (51 percent).</b> Inputs that rely on placeholder text, which vanishes as the user types and is often not announced. Tell the agent: "Every input needs a visible label linked to it."</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">4</span><div><b>Empty links (46.3 percent).</b> Icon-only links, such as a logo or a social icon, with no text a screen reader can say. Tell the agent: "Every link needs an accessible name."</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">5</span><div><b>Empty buttons (30.6 percent).</b> The close X, the hamburger menu, the heart icon. Tell the agent: "Every icon button needs an aria-label that says what it does."</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">6</span><div><b>Missing page language (13.5 percent).</b> Without it, a screen reader may read English with the wrong pronunciation rules. Tell the agent: "Set the lang attribute on the html element."</div></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p>Two newer WCAG 2.2 rules catch apps in particular. Touch targets should be at least 24 by 24 pixels, so tiny icon buttons fail, and a focused element must not be completely hidden behind a sticky header or a cookie banner. Both are easy for an agent to fix once named, and both are invisible to a founder who only ever uses a mouse.</p>

      <div class="cav">Three tests, one afternoon</div>
      <h2 id="test">How do I test accessibility if I cannot read code?</h2>
      <p>Use three free tests that together cover far more than any single tool. Automated scanners are fast but partial: Deque, which makes the axe engine, reports that its automated testing finds about 57 percent of issues by volume, and a UK Government Digital Service experiment that planted 143 barriers on one page found the best single tool caught only 37 to 41 percent, with 29 percent missed by every tool tested. A keyboard and a screen reader fill most of the gap.</p>
      <div class="sec">
        <ul class="pillars">
          <li><span class="dot">1</span><div><b>The keyboard-only test.</b> Put the mouse away. Press Tab to move through the page and Enter or Space to activate things. Every link, button, menu, and form field should be reachable, in a sensible order, with a visible outline showing where you are. If you get stuck, lose track of focus, or cannot close a pop-up, keyboard and switch users hit the same wall.</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">2</span><div><b>The screen reader test.</b> Turn on VoiceOver (built into every Mac and iPhone) or install NVDA, which is free and open source for Windows. Close your eyes and try to sign up, find the price, and complete the main task. Listen for buttons announced as just "button," images announced as file names, and fields with no name.</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">3</span><div><b>The automated scan.</b> Run the free axe DevTools or WAVE browser extension, or the Lighthouse accessibility audit built into Chrome, on every key screen, not just the home page. Fix everything it flags, then repeat tests 1 and 2, because a clean scan is where testing starts, not where it ends.</div></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Cost</th><th>Catches</th><th>Misses</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="band">Automated scan (axe, WAVE, Lighthouse)</td><td>Free</td><td>Contrast, missing alt, empty buttons and links, missing labels</td><td>Meaning: alt text that exists but says nothing useful</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">Keyboard only</td><td>Free</td><td>Focus traps, unreachable menus, invisible focus, broken pop-ups</td><td>What a screen reader announces</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">Screen reader (VoiceOver, NVDA)</td><td>Free</td><td>Confusing names, reading order, unlabeled controls in context</td><td>Visual issues such as contrast</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">Professional audit</td><td>Paid</td><td>Full WCAG review with real assistive technology users</td><td>Little, if done well</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>For apps that hold payments, health, or other sensitive tasks, or that serve a large public audience, Best Answer Hub suggests budgeting for a professional audit once the three free tests pass. A Lighthouse score of 100 is a useful signal, but it scores only automated audits, so it can sit on top of an app a blind user still cannot finish.</p>
      <blockquote>An agent saying "accessible" is a claim. <span class="accent">A clean scan plus a keyboard run plus a screen reader run is evidence.</span></blockquote>

      <div class="cav">The shortcut that is not</div>
      <h2 id="widgets">Will an accessibility overlay widget protect my app?</h2>
      <p>No reliable evidence says it will, and a federal regulator has penalized the biggest name selling that promise. Overlays are scripts that add a toolbar or automated fixes on top of a site. In April 2025 the US Federal Trade Commission approved a final order requiring accessiBe to pay $1 million, alleging it falsely claimed its widget could make any website compliant with WCAG and formatted third-party reviews to look independent. UsableNet's 2025 data shows lawsuits filed every month against companies already using such widgets.</p>
      <p>The accessibility community is blunt about it: the Overlay Fact Sheet, signed by more than 1,000 accessibility practitioners, advocates removing overlays altogether, largely because screen reader users already have their own tools and the overlay does not change the underlying code they read. Best Answer Hub recommends spending the widget's subscription money on fixing the six errors above instead.</p>

      <div class="cav">Beyond the US</div>
      <h2 id="outside">Does this apply if my business is outside the US?</h2>
      <p>US lawsuits follow sales more than headquarters. A 2017 New York federal suit named an Italian company over its website, though that company also ran a New York showroom. New York federal courts have since held that a website merely reachable from the state is not enough for a lawsuit there, while actual sales or deliveries to New York residents can be. A founder anywhere who sells to US customers should treat the risk as real and ask a lawyer about specific exposure.</p>
      <p>Europe has its own rule. The European Accessibility Act has applied since 28 June 2025 to e-commerce and other consumer services sold in the EU, and in-scope services must publish information on how they meet the accessibility requirements. Microenterprises providing services, meaning fewer than 10 staff and no more than EUR 2 million in annual turnover or balance sheet, are exempt from those requirements, which covers most solo founders. Products are not exempt, and growth can end the exemption, so Best Answer Hub suggests building to WCAG 2.2 AA now rather than retrofitting later.</p>
      <div class="callout"><div class="h">The other half of the risk</div><p>Accessibility is one of several ways a vibe-coded app draws legal attention without ever being hacked. Tracking scripts, session recording, marketing texts and emails, children's sign-ups, and subscription checkouts each carry their own rules and penalties. The companion Best Answer Hub playbook, <a href="/playbooks/can-a-vibe-coded-app-get-you-sued/">Can Your Vibe-Coded App Get You Sued?</a>, fact-checks those claims with the real figures.</p></div>

      <div class="cav">Do these today</div>
      <h2 id="week">What should I do about accessibility this week?</h2>
      <p>Five actions close most of the common gaps in an afternoon, none require reading code, and each ends with something you can see or hear yourself. They map to the errors that appear in demand letters most often, and to the compliance dimension of the readiness check below.</p>
      <div class="sec">
        <ul class="pillars">
          <li><span class="dot">1</span><div><b>Give your agent the six-rule standing order.</b> Paste the six instructions from the checklist above into your agent's project rules file, such as CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, so they apply to every future screen, not just today's fix.</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">2</span><div><b>Scan every key screen.</b> Sign-up, sign-in, pricing, checkout, and the main task. Ask the agent to fix every axe violation and show you the clean rerun. Passing looks like zero violations on each screen, with the scan saved.</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">3</span><div><b>Do one keyboard-only run.</b> Complete sign-up and the main task without touching the mouse. Passing looks like reaching every control with a visible focus outline and no traps.</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">4</span><div><b>Do one screen reader run.</b> With VoiceOver or NVDA, complete the same task with your eyes closed. Passing looks like every button and field announcing a clear name.</div></li>
          <li><span class="dot">5</span><div><b>Remove any overlay widget and add a contact route.</b> Replace the widget with real fixes, and publish an email address where users can report a barrier. A user who can reach you is a user who may not need a lawyer.</div></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p>Those five turn "my agent says it is accessible" into evidence you produced yourself. The next step is seeing where accessibility sits among everything else an app needs before strangers rely on it, which is what the free score below measures.</p>

      <div class="cta">
        <span class="kicker">A letter costs more than a fix.</span>
        <h3>Take the free Vibe Coder Readiness Check</h3>
        <p>45 questions across nine dimensions, including compliance, accessibility, and privacy, with a 0 to 100 score in about 10 to 12 minutes. No signup and no email, and the score is never paywalled.</p>
        <a class="btn" href="/knowledge-center/vibe-coder/">Score your app now</a>
      </div>

      <div class="cav">Good questions</div>
